## Pull request overview

Redesigns list/object view toolbars to follow a more compact Airtable-style pattern by consolidating ListView controls into a compact toolbar and stripping ObjectView’s duplicate toolbar controls.

**Changes:**
- Reworked `ListView` toolbar UI: compact tool buttons on the left + expandable Search on the right (with optional view tabs row).
- Simplified `ObjectView` toolbar to only show named view tabs / view switcher / create button, delegating filter/sort/search to `ListView`.
- Updated unit tests to reflect the new Search interaction and the removal of ObjectView search.

### Reviewed changes

Copilot reviewed 4 out of 4 changed files in this pull request and generated 5 comments.

| File | Description |
| ---- | ----------- |
| packages/plugin-list/src/ListView.tsx | Implements new compact toolbar with tool buttons + expandable search UI. |
| packages/plugin-view/src/ObjectView.tsx | Removes duplicate toolbar controls from ObjectView and trims imports/UI. |
| packages/plugin-list/src/__tests__/ListView.test.tsx | Updates tests for “search button → expand → input” flow. |
| packages/plugin-view/src/__tests__/ObjectView.test.tsx | Updates expectations to assert ObjectView no longer renders search. |


<details>
<summary>Comments suppressed due to low confidence (1)</summary>

**packages/plugin-view/src/__tests__/ObjectView.test.tsx:128**
* These tests assert that ObjectView never renders a search box (even when `showSearch` is provided). Since `showSearch` is still part of the `ObjectViewSchema` type, this effectively enshrines a breaking behavior change. Please either (1) keep search support in ObjectView when used standalone and add a test covering `showSearch: true`, or (2) remove/deprecate `showSearch` from the schema/types and update docs/tests accordingly.
```
    it('should not render search box (delegated to ListView toolbar)', () => {
      const schema: ObjectViewSchema = {
        type: 'object-view',
        objectName: 'contacts',
      };

      render(<ObjectView schema={schema} dataSource={mockDataSource} />);

      expect(screen.queryByPlaceholderText(/search/i)).toBeNull();
    });

    it('should not render search box when showSearch is false', () => {
      const schema: ObjectViewSchema = {
        type: 'object-view',
        objectName: 'contacts',
        showSearch: false,
      };

      render(<ObjectView schema={schema} dataSource={mockDataSource} />);

      expect(screen.queryByPlaceholderText(/search/i)).toBeNull();
    });
```
</details>

This test queries the Search control by accessible name, but the implementation currently derives that name from text that is visually hidden via Tailwind's `hidden` class (CSS not applied in jsdom). Once the button becomes icon-only on small screens, the accessible name may be empty unless an `aria-label`/`sr-only` label is added. Consider asserting against an explicit `aria-label` (and add it in the component) so the test reflects real accessibility behavior.
```suggestion
    expect(searchButton).toBeInTheDocument();
    // Ensure the search control exposes a stable accessible name via aria-label
    expect(searchButton).toHaveAttribute('aria-label', expect.stringMatching(/search/i));
```
